{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-helena-izates", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-15-01-circumcision-taught", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 20.17-48) tells how the merchant Ananias and a Galilean Jew taught Izates that a convert was not complete 'unless he were circumcised', and Queen Helena and Izates embraced the Jewish customs in exactly this period - Gentile conversion to Judaism with circumcision as the disputed point, not these Judean teachers or their Antioch mission."}
{"from": "text:josephus-antiquities-20", "type": "attests", "to": "person:james-brother-of-jesus", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Antiquities 20.9.1 (c. 200, the passage commonly cited as 20.200): Ananus the high priest convened the sanhedrin and condemned to death 'the brother of Jesus, who was called Christ, whose name was James' (c. 62 CE) - independent naming of the same man who speaks in Acts 15:13. It says nothing of any presiding role at this meeting."}
{"from": "text:josephus-apion-2-weekly-assembly", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-15-21-moses-sabbath-read", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (Against Apion 2): Moses' law permits the people to leave their work 'and to assemble together for the hearing of the law ... every week' - weekly assemblies to hear the law read, in the period. It does not say every city had Moses preached, nor name synagogues."}
{"from": "text:talmud-sanhedrin-56a", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-15-20-decree-abstentions", "grade": "D", "note": "Sanhedrin 56a (Soncino): 'seven precepts were the sons of Noah commanded: social laws' ... 'to refrain from blasphemy, idolatry; adultery; bloodshed; robbery; and eating flesh cut from a living animal' - a rabbinic universal list overlapping the decree's items. Redacted c. 500 CE, its relationship to the Apostolic Decree is disputed (fetched Wikipedia 'Council of Jerusalem': 'modern scholars dispute the connection'); graded D as a later reception."}
{"from": "text:tosefta-avodah-zarah-9-4", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-15-20-decree-abstentions", "grade": "D", "note": "The Tosefta (Avodah Zarah 9:4 in the Sefaria/Steinsaltz numbering) carries the earliest complete rabbinic version of the seven Noahide laws per the fetched Wikipedia 'Seven Laws of Noah' - the tradition the brief points to for the decree's parallels. Redacted c. 200-250 CE; the connection to Acts 15 is disputed, so D."}
{"from": "artifact:4q174-florilegium", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-15-14-18-amos-quoted", "grade": "C", "note": "4Q174, the pre-Christian Qumran 'Florilegium' (Herodian period), carries Amos 9:11 (the fallen tabernacle of David that James quotes) among its contents per the fetched List of the Dead Sea Scrolls; a pre-Christian manuscript carrying the exact quoted passage is B (docs/calibration.md). It carries 9:11 only, not the Gentiles line of 9:12, and attests the passage's currency, not James's use of it. Regraded B to C by the architect: 4Q174 carries Amos 9:11 but not 9:12, so it is not the whole quotation (the Isaiah-scroll rule), and 4Q174 is C by precedent."}
{"from": "passage:ac-15-01", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:genesis-17-12", "note": "Genesis 17:12 commands circumcision of every male child as the covenant sign ('the custom of Moses' of Acts 15:1 rests on the Torah). Biblical cross-reference, not evidence (docs/calibration.md)."}
{"from": "passage:ac-15-16", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:amos-9-11-12", "note": "Acts 15:16-18 quotes Amos 9:11-12 (WEB: 'I will raise up the tent of David who is fallen ... all the nations who are called by my name'). Biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:ac-15-20-decree-abstentions", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:leviticus-17-18", "note": "Leviticus 17-18 forbids sacrificing to idols/goat demons (17:7-9), eating blood (17:10-14, which underlies 'what is strangled'), and the sexual immoralities of ch. 18 - the Torah core the fourfold decree recalls. Biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "passage:ac-15-20", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:leviticus-17-18", "note": "Same Leviticus 17-18 background for the letter's version of the decree (15:29 repeats it). Biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:ac-15-06-apostles-elders-gathered", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:galatians-2-1-10", "note": "Paul's own account, Galatians 2:1-10, describes a Jerusalem meeting with the pillars James, Cephas and John about Gentile obligation - generally considered the same event (fetched Wikipedia 'Council of Jerusalem'), though scholars disagree and the accounts differ: Galatians 2 names no decree or four abstentions, has Titus uncompelled, recalls a private meeting and the 'remember the poor' request. Biblical cross-reference, never evidence (docs/calibration.md); the differences are recorded here, not as complicates."}
